  "label": "the overallintent",
  "description": "meme-id: meme_image; hasOverallIntent: overall-intent_description; overall-intent-description: The scene depicts a diverse group of six professionals (five women and one man) posing confidently in a modern office setting. The text overlaid on the image reads: 'it didn't happen because of the actions of one man' and 'it happened because of people who were just doing their jobs'. The overall intent of this meme is to shift blame away from a single individual (often implied to be a man) and attribute collective responsibility to a group of people who are portrayed as merely performing their duties. This is a common rhetorical device used to deflect accountability, often in the context of workplace misconduct, corporate scandals, or social issues where a single person is scapegoated. The meme's intent is propagandistic, aiming to influence perception by suggesting that systemic or organizational failure is the result of collective inaction rather than individual malfeasance. It does not contain explicit abusive, cyberbullying, harmful, hateful, misogynous, or other forms of toxicity as defined in the provided knowledge base. The message is framed as a commentary on accountability and group responsibility, not as an attack on any specific community or individual based on protected characteristics. The visual of a diverse, professional group is used to analogically support the textual message that 'everyone was just doing their jobs,' implying a collective apathy or complicity.",
